---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Lists</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 1.23 &ndash; 1.26. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> Arrays</h2>

          <p>
            One name, many values, kept in order. You reach any
            single value by its index, and the first index is
            0.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Array</dt>
            <dd>An ordered collection of values stored under one name.</dd>
            <dt>Element</dt>
            <dd>One value inside an array.</dd>
            <dt>Index</dt>
            <dd>
            The position of an element, counted from 0 in JavaScript.
            </dd>
            <dt>Reference</dt>
            <dd>
            A name that points at an array rather than holding its own
            copy.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> Length and Safe Access</h2>

          <p>
            The last valid index is always one
            less than the length. Reading past the end does not crash &mdash;
            it hands back{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="undefined" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline
            />, which is easier to miss.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt><CodeSnippet code="length" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></dt>
            <dd>How many elements an array currently holds.</dd>
            <dt>Off-by-one error</dt>
            <dd>
            A mistake of exactly one position, usually at the start or end
            of a range.
            </dd>
            <dt><CodeSnippet code="undefined" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></dt>
            <dd>What JavaScript returns when a value does not exist.</dd>
            <dt>Bounds check</dt>
            <dd>Testing that an index is valid before using it.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Walking Through an Array</h2>

          <p>
            Loops and arrays belong together.
            Use an indexed loop when you need the position, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="for...of" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline
            /> when you only need the value.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Traversal</dt>
            <dd>Visiting every element of an array in order.</dd>
            <dt>Indexed loop</dt>
            <dd>A traversal that uses a counter to reach each position.</dd>
            <dt>
            <CodeSnippet code="for...of" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />
            </dt>
            <dd>A loop that gives you each value directly.</dd>
            <dt>Filtering</dt>
            <dd>
            Building a new collection from the items that meet a
            condition.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> p5 Lab 6: Drawing From Data</h2>

          <p>
            A chart is a traversal. The
            numbers decide the shape, so changing the data changes the
            picture with no other edits.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Data-driven drawing</dt>
            <dd>
            A picture whose shape comes from values, not from hard-coded
            numbers.
            </dd>
            <dt>Parallel arrays</dt>
            <dd>
            Two or more arrays where the same index refers to the same
            thing.
            </dd>
            <dt>Visualization</dt>
            <dd>Representing data so a person can see it at a glance.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
